  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+2x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x-2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$",
